  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nA varying degree of northwesterly flow will prevail aloft across the \nMiddle Mississippi Valley through a majority of the long-range \nforecast period per both ensemble clusters and deterministic \nguidance. This leads to high confidence in our weather staying \nseasonable and calm through a majority of the upcoming workweek.\n\nToward the end of the workweek, guidance shows gradual height rises \nahead of a ridge building into the Midwest next weekend; however, \nhow quickly heights rise and by how much is uncertain. This has the \nspread between the 25th and 75th percentile of temperatures among \nensemble guidance increasing by as much as 10 degrees Wednesday \nonward. While the entire ensemble suite drifts warmer Wednesday and \nbeyond, the spread leads to uncertainty by how much. The warmer \nsolution that the NBM favors, most likely because of the warm-biased \nGFS/GEFS, has temperatures running 10-15 degrees above normal \nthrough this portion of the period. However, the cooler side of \nensemble spread has temperatures at to a few degrees above \nclimatology. All of this to say, confidence is high in temperatures \nwarming above normal by next weekend, but by how much is\nuncertain.",
